# Revision History # {#revisions}

R0: Initial revision.

# Introduction # {#intro}

We propose <code>&#91;&#91;@expression]]</code> to require a library-defined operation when a class or function is defined. A serialization library can then check field annotations and derive a schema at the definition site.

[[P3394R4]] supplies stored annotations and explores callbacks in section 4.5. [[P0707R5]] proposes <code>class(metafunc)</code> syntax for applying compile-time transformations to class definitions. This design provides:

1. Explicit application, with each decorator receiving the previous decorator's output.
2. Computed metadata attached to the returned definition.

The examples use the reflection facilities in [[P2996R13]]. They are design sketches: definition replacement and the <code>stdx::meta</code> interfaces require new language and library support.

# The decorator closest to the definition runs first # {#proposal}

A decorator receives a value representing a definition and returns a value representing the next definition. Each call must be a constant expression even if no metadata is queried. <code>&#91;&#91;=constant-expression]]</code> supplies passive annotations.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
[[@schema::derive]]
[[@schema::validate]]
struct User
{
    [[=schema::Field{}]] int id;
    [[=schema::Field{}]] std::string name;
};
</xmp>

<code>schema::validate</code> runs first. <code>schema::derive</code> receives the validated definition and returns the final definition of <code>User</code>.

# Replacement needs an inspectable definition # {#representation}

Decorators need a representation they can inspect through reflection or replace using tokens. The compiler installs the returned definition at the original definition site. The examples use <code>std::meta::info</code> as a placeholder for this representation:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
auto class_type = stdx::meta::as_class(definition);
auto function = stdx::meta::as_function(definition);
auto body = stdx::meta::body_of(function);
</xmp>

<code>as_class</code> exposes the complete class, <code>as_function</code> the function, and <code>body_of</code> the function's body tokens. Converting constructed tokens into an inspectable definition must preserve what names refer to and where the tokens came from.

It remains unresolved when replacement occurs and whether reflections before and after replacement identify the same entities. Rules are also needed for queries within the class, references to removed members, template instantiation, and generated definitions.

# Validation retains computed metadata # {#metadata}

The schema helpers inspect public members using their own access permissions. Each <code>Field</code> remains an input annotation, separate from the computed descriptor:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
namespace schema
{
    struct Field
    {
        const char* name = nullptr;
    };

    struct FieldInfo
    {
        const char* name;
        std::meta::info type;
    };

    struct SchemaInfo
    {
        std::meta::info fields;
    };
}
</xmp>

<code>FieldInfo</code> records the resolved name and reflected type. A null <code>Field::name</code> selects the declared name without changing the input annotation.

The omitted library helpers reject misplaced <code>Field</code> annotations and empty or duplicate names. <code>describe_fields</code> preserves declaration order and stores names in static storage with <code>std::define_static_string</code> [[P3491R3]].

<xmp class=language-cpp>
namespace schema
{
    consteval std::meta::info validate(std::meta::info definition)
    {
        check_fields(stdx::meta::as_class(definition));
        return definition;
    }

    consteval std::meta::info derive(std::meta::info definition)
    {
        auto fields = describe_fields(stdx::meta::as_class(definition));
        auto descriptor = SchemaInfo{
            std::meta::reflect_constant_array(fields)
        };

        return stdx::meta::with_annotation(
            definition,
            std::meta::reflect_constant(descriptor));
    }
}
</xmp>

<code>validate</code> returns its input unchanged. <code>with_annotation</code> attaches the reflected <code>SchemaInfo</code> constant to that definition. <code>reflect_constant_array</code> returns a reflection of an array of field descriptors stored as a template parameter object. <code>SchemaInfo::fields</code> contains that reflection.

Failed validation or descriptor construction makes the program ill-formed. Diagnostics identify the application and checked declaration.

After definition, consumers retrieve the stored schema:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
constexpr auto annotation = std::meta::annotations_of_with_type(
    ^^User, ^^schema::SchemaInfo)[0];

constexpr auto descriptor =
    std::meta::extract<schema::SchemaInfo>(annotation);
</xmp>

The query does not repeat derivation.

# A class validator checks annotations across members # {#primary-keys}

The primary-key validator counts annotations across public non-static data members and reports errors with <code>std::meta::exception</code> [[P3560R2]]. <code>access_context::current()</code> applies the access rules where the helper is defined, without inheriting the caller's privileges.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
namespace schema
{
    struct PrimaryKey {};

    consteval std::meta::info validate_primary_key(std::meta::info definition)
    {
        auto class_type = stdx::meta::as_class(definition);
        auto access = std::meta::access_context::current();
        std::size_t count = 0;

        for (auto member : std::meta::nonstatic_data_members_of(class_type, access))
            count += std::meta::annotations_of_with_type(member, ^^PrimaryKey).size();

        if (count > 1)
            throw std::meta::exception("At most one primary key is permitted", class_type);

        return definition;
    }
}

[[@schema::validate_primary_key]]
struct Invoice
{
    [[=schema::PrimaryKey{}]] int invoice_id;
    std::string customer;
};

[[@schema::validate_primary_key]]
struct Batch
{
    [[=schema::PrimaryKey{}]] int batch_id, sequence;
};
</xmp>

<code>Invoice</code> passes. <code>Batch</code> fails because its annotation applies to both declarators. Repeated annotations on one member also fail.

# Guard validation can find members declared later # {#guarded-members}

<code>GuardedBy</code> can name a public non-static data member declared later in the class:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
namespace locking
{
    struct GuardedBy
    {
        const char* member_name;

        consteval GuardedBy(std::string_view name)
            : member_name(std::define_static_string(name))
        {
        }
    };

    consteval std::meta::info validate_guards(std::meta::info definition)
    {
        auto class_type = stdx::meta::as_class(definition);
        auto members = std::meta::nonstatic_data_members_of(
            class_type, std::meta::access_context::current());

        for (auto member : members)
            for (auto annotation : std::meta::annotations_of_with_type(member, ^^GuardedBy))
            {
                auto guard = std::meta::extract<GuardedBy>(annotation);
                bool found = false;
                for (auto candidate : members)
                    if (std::meta::has_identifier(candidate)
                        && std::meta::identifier_of(candidate) == guard.member_name)
                        found = true;

                if (!found)
                    throw std::meta::exception("GuardedBy names a missing member", member);
            }

        return definition;
    }
}

[[@locking::validate_guards]]
struct Connection
{
    [[=locking::GuardedBy{"mutex"}]] int refcount = 0;
    [[=locking::GuardedBy{"mutex"}]] bool connected = false;
    std::mutex mutex;
};
</xmp>

Both <code>GuardedBy</code> annotations name <code>Connection::mutex</code>. Missing or misspelled member names fail validation. The check does not inspect guard types or locking within function bodies.

# Configured decorators check function signatures # {#commands}

<code>cli::Command</code> accepts a non-member function of type <code>void(command_context&amp;)</code> or <code>void(command_context&amp;) noexcept</code>. Comparing function types also rejects variadic functions.

<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct command_context
{
    std::ostream& out;
};

namespace cli
{
    struct CommandInfo
    {
        const char* name;
        bool is_noexcept;
    };

    struct Command
    {
        const char* name;

        consteval Command(std::string_view command_name)
            : name(std::define_static_string(command_name))
        {
        }

        consteval std::meta::info operator()(std::meta::info definition) const
        {
            using signature = void(command_context&);
            using nothrow_signature = void(command_context&) noexcept;
            auto function = stdx::meta::as_function(definition);
            auto function_type = std::meta::type_of(function);
            if (std::meta::is_class_member(function)
                || (!std::meta::is_same_type(function_type, ^^signature)
                    && !std::meta::is_same_type(function_type, ^^nothrow_signature)))
                throw std::meta::exception(
                    "Command requires a non-member void(command_context&) function", function);

            return stdx::meta::with_annotation(
                definition,
                std::meta::reflect_constant(CommandInfo{name, std::meta::is_noexcept(function)}));
        }
    };
}

[[@cli::Command{"status"}]]
void status(command_context& context)
{
    context.out << "Ready\n";
}
</xmp>

The body remains unchanged. <code>CommandInfo</code> stores the configured name <code>"status"</code> and a false <code>is_noexcept</code> value.

# Composition retains intermediate facts # {#composition}

A decorator can call other decorators and pass each returned definition to the next. The omitted schema helpers count fields and remove transient fields before the final schema is derived:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct SchemaChange
{
    std::size_t fields_before;
    std::size_t fields_after;
};

consteval std::meta::info prepare_schema(std::meta::info definition)
{
    definition = schema::validate(definition);
    auto before = schema::field_count(definition);

    definition = schema::remove_transient_fields(definition);
    auto after = schema::field_count(definition);
    definition = schema::derive(definition);

    return stdx::meta::with_annotation(
        definition,
        std::meta::reflect_constant(SchemaChange{before, after}));
}
</xmp>

The output retains the final schema and both field counts so consumers need not reconstruct the earlier definition.

Each transform specifies which annotations it preserves or recomputes. Computing final metadata after changes to the definition keeps that metadata consistent. <code>SchemaChange</code> records intermediate facts separately from the final schema.

# Stored schemas can define companion aggregates # {#companion-type}

A separate namespace-scope <code>consteval</code> block can use the schema to define a companion aggregate:

<xmp class=language-cpp>
struct UserRow;

consteval
{
    constexpr auto annotation = std::meta::annotations_of_with_type(
        ^^User, ^^schema::SchemaInfo)[0];
    constexpr auto descriptor = std::meta::extract<schema::SchemaInfo>(annotation);

    std::vector<std::meta::info> members;
    for (auto field : [:descriptor.fields:])
        members.push_back(std::meta::data_member_spec(field.type, {.name = field.name}));

    std::meta::define_aggregate(^^UserRow, members);
}

static_assert(std::is_same_v<decltype(UserRow::id), int>);
</xmp>

<code>UserRow</code> uses the schema's names, types, and declaration order. Serialization names containing punctuation need separate C++ identifiers. Matching the schema does not require matching <code>User</code>'s object layout.

# Alternatives # {#alternatives}

## "Call the validator explicitly" ## {#explicit-call}

A separate <code>consteval</code> block can call the validator. The author must keep that call with the definition. Applying a decorator makes the call part of the definition.

## "Validate when metadata is queried" ## {#query-validation}

A library accessor can compute a descriptor on request. If nobody queries the metadata, that check never runs. Mandatory decorator evaluation also diagnoses errors in those unqueried definitions.

# Conclusion # {#conclusion}

Explicit decorators let libraries validate or transform definitions and retain computed annotations. Serialization and command libraries can build on the same composition rule. Replacement timing and identity need specification before adoption wording can be written.

We request direction on proceeding with <code>&#91;&#91;@expression]]</code> applications where each decorator returns the definition passed to the next.

# Disclosure # {#disclosure}

The author provides information and serves at the pleasure of the committee. This revision requests design direction and provides no implementation results.

